session:claudeSonnet5 a prov:SoftwareAgent ;
    rdfs:label "Claude Sonnet 5" .

session:pr651 a agp:AgentSession ;
    prov:wasAssociatedWith session:claudeSonnet5 ;
    prov:used ghissues:631 ;
    prov:startedAtTime "2026-09-01T14:05:20+02:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-09-08T15:28:28Z"^^xsd:dateTime .

session:pr651Summary a agp:TranscriptSummary ;
    agp:summaryText "Issue #631's own body already stated the deferred constraints (sh:datatype, the four range constraints, sh:pattern, sh:lessThan/sh:lessThanOrEquals) were 'not safely portable today' without first fixing gaps in sparql_parser's expression evaluator relative to shacl's own comparator/lexical helpers. The task brief framed this as a straightforward port of that remaining #62 scope into shacl/src/translate.rs and evaluate.rs; orientation established the issue's own analysis was authoritative (Status: ready/reviewed) and that the real work was mostly in sparql_parser, not shacl. Read PR #632's merged diff to extract the exact FilterAtom rule-generation pattern (NodeKind/LanguageIn arms in translate.rs, using RuleAtom::FilterAtom wrapping a sparql_parser::ast::Expression, relying on eval_expression_bool's Unary(Not,_) treating an inner comparison error as false-then-negated-to-true for 'incomparable => violation' semantics) before writing anything. Traced sh:lessThan/sh:lessThanOrEquals as a genuinely different kind of blocked (#343's per-(value,otherValue)-pair discriminated-predicate requirement is an expressiveness gap, not a fidelity gap) and split it into a separate follow-up issue (#637) rather than attempting it here. For the remaining three: rewrote compare_graph_elements to classify each literal operand into Numeric/Str/Bool/Date/DateTime/DateTimeNaive (mirroring shacl::evaluate::SparqlCmpValue) instead of falling back to naive lexical-text comparison across mismatched datatypes; widened graph_element_to_string to cover every RdfLiteral variant's lexical form (needed by both STR() and REGEX()'s text argument, since REGEX(STR(v),...) is the natural FilterAtom encoding of sh:pattern per SHACL's own str($value) semantics); and added a new non-standard internal function IS_LEXICALLY_VALID(value,dtIri) — deliberately NOT changing DATATYPE()'s own spec-correct semantics or the existing xsd:*(...) cast functions' established lenient behavior, since sh:datatype's #325 well-formedness requirement (ill-formed lexical form under a matching nominal type must still violate) is SHACL-specific, not a generic SPARQL DATATYPE() concern. Ported sh:datatype/range/pattern to translate.rs FilterAtom rules using these three primitives, removed the corresponding eval_prop_constraint arms in evaluate.rs (leaving has_datatype/is_well_formed_lexical/bound_to_comparable/range_violates/regex_with_flags/lexical_form in place, unchanged, since constraint_conforms — the recursive sh:and/or/not/xone/node/qualifiedValueShape checker — still calls them directly, same pattern #632 established for matches_node_kind/lang_matches). Hit the CLAUDE.md-documented cross-worktree shared-target-dir fingerprint hazard twice during verification (a stale rdf_owl_translator compile error unrelated to this diff's actual source, and an 11-test shacl_suite failure batch that vanished after touching the changed files and rebuilding) — both resolved exactly per the documented workaround, not real regressions. Final state: 175/175 shacl_suite, 10/10 w3c_shacl_suite (no skip-list changes), 146/146 sparql12_suite (3 new regression tests for the comparator/STR/REGEX fidelity fixes) all pass; full cargo test --workspace green except one pre-existing sparql_endpoint query_timeout test unrelated to this diff, SIGKILLed under the shared machine's concurrent build load; fmt and clippy -D warnings clean; all 8 CI jobs pass." ;
    agp:reasoningFor ghpull:651 ;
    agp:decisionPoint session:pr651Decision ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy session:pr651 ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo session:claudeSonnet5 .

session:pr651Decision a agp:Decision ;
    agp:summaryText "Added IS_LEXICALLY_VALID as a new non-standard internal sparql_parser function (dispatched only in eval_function_bool, not a real SPARQL/XPath builtin) rather than making DATATYPE() itself check lexical well-formedness or widening the existing xsd:*(...) cast functions' semantics to double as validity checks." ;
    agp:alternative "Considered making the existing xsd:integer(...)/xsd:byte(...)-style cast functions serve double duty as the well-formedness check (e.g. FILTER(BOUND(xsd:byte(v)))), which would have meant either changing DATATYPE()'s SPARQL-spec-correct semantics (it normatively only reports the nominal type IRI, never lexical validity) or silently tightening the established lenient casting behavior other SPARQL FILTER/BIND expressions already depend on. Also considered a pure-regex well-formedness encoding built once at rule-generation time, but the real W3C SHACL suite fixture (datatype-ill-formed, xsd:byte with '300' out of i8 range) needs an actual bounded-integer-type parse, not just a digit-shape regex, so a regex-only approach would have silently accepted out-of-range values. Rejected both in favor of a small, clearly-scoped internal extension that ports shacl::evaluate::is_well_formed_lexical's exact per-datatype-IRI logic without touching any spec-defined SPARQL function's behavior." .
